{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when I cloud a cloud over the earth, and the bow shall be seen in the cloud.</b> Regarding the matter of the bow, opinions abound and perfect commentators as well as many others have been uncertain about it. For according to the simple reading, it would seem that the bow is now newly created and signals the new covenant after YHWH made it with every living thing, while our eyes see that the bow is a natural thing, born from the sun when it strikes the moist air. And Rabbi Isaac (Mahari) explained that even before this, the bow used to come forth from the rays of the sun, but it was not visible to creatures at all — not in the clouds and not in the air — for from the beginning of creation the vapors that rose from the earth were dense and very thick because of the strength of the earth, as it is written (Genesis 2:6), ״וְאֵד יַעֲלֶה מִן הָאָרֶץ וְהִשְׁקָה אֶת פְּנֵי הָאֲדָמָה״ — for even a single vapor rising from the earth was sufficient to water all the ground. And because of the great abundance of those waters in the air, the sun's rays could not pass through them, with the result that the bow made no impression, not in the clouds and not in the air. And this was the cause of the Flood — I mean the abundance of the waters within the vapors. And by the mercy of HaShem, blessed be He, after the Flood He diminished the vapors and refined the air, until the sun's rays were able to pass through the air, and then the bow became visible in the cloud to creatures, and this served them as a sign of the diminution of the waters in the air, so that no flood would come through them again to destroy all flesh. And what is said, ״וּרְאִיתִיהָ לִזְכֹּר בְּרִית עוֹלָם״, is explained as ״וְהִרְאִיתִיהָ״ — I shall show it — just as ״עַתָּה יָדַעְתִּי כִּי יְרֵא אֱלֹהִים אַתָּה״ is explained by the Rambam as ״עַתָּה הוֹדַעְתִּי״ — now I have made known. And to this opinion the Ramban also inclined, except that he wrote that at first the bow appeared in the air and not in the cloud, and after the Flood it appeared also in the cloud. And a difficulty presses upon their explanation from what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Jerusalemite Berakhot 65a; Bavli Ketubot 77b) — that in the days of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi the bow was not seen. If so, the air reverted to its former state, and after their death it returned to being pure and refined as at first. But these are strange things for the intellect to accept — that the air should change at several different times." "And what seems close to me, to resolve this doubt, is that since they said that in the days of those pious ones the bow was not seen, they did not say that there was no bow at all. Rather, there certainly was a bow, but it was not seen — for the creatures were not inquiring after it and did not exert themselves to look at it; therefore it was not seen. For every generation that feared the punishments brought on by the wickedness of its inhabitants, since the protection of the righteous had been removed and there were no righteous people in the generation to shield them — in those generations they fixed their eyes on the sign of the bow and would scrutinize it, to see whether the vapors had changed through their sin to revert to what they had been before the Flood. And their eyes were always lifted toward this sign, to trust in the sign that no punishment would be brought upon the world. But those pious ones — Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi — who shielded the people of their generation, so that all creatures relied on the merit of these elders, whose virtue would stand for them to protect them: therefore they had no need to look at the bow, for they were confident that the covenant that YHWH had cut with all flesh after the Flood would not change on their account." "And this is what is said, ״וְהָיָה בְּעַנְנִי עָנָן עַל הָאָרֶץ״ — Rashi explained: 'when I think to bring darkness and destruction upon the world,' etc. What did Rashi see to explain ״בְּעַנְנִי עָנָן״ as darkness and destruction — is it not a rain-cloud, according to the simple sense? Rather, it was certainly a difficulty for Rashi: for if its meaning is a rain-cloud, it would imply that in every rain-cloud the bow would be visible — and this is not so, for in the generation of the righteous the bow is not seen. Rather, this is his explanation: at the time when I think to bring darkness and destruction upon the world — and this is specifically at a time when no righteous people are present in the world — then ״וְנִרְאֲתָה הַקֶּשֶׁת בֶּעָנָן״, the creatures will look at the bow and fix their eyes on this sign, for through it they are confident that no flood will be brought upon the world. And this is what it says: ״וּרְאִיתִיהָ לִזְכֹּר בְּרִית עוֹלָם״ (Genesis 9:16): I set My eyes on the sign of the covenant that I gave to all flesh, not to bring a flood even upon a generation that is entirely guilty, who deserve that darkness and destruction be brought upon them. But at a time when I am not thinking to bring darkness and destruction — that is, at a time when righteous people are present in the generation — then the bow is not seen in the cloud, for the creatures do not look after it. And I too see the deeds of the righteous and restrain the attribute of judgment from bringing darkness and destruction, and I have no need to set My eyes on the sign of the covenant of the bow. And perhaps it is along the lines of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Chagigah 16a): ״כָּל הַמִּסְתַּכֵּל בַּקֶּשֶׁת אֵינוֹ חָס עַל כְּבוֹד קוֹנוֹ״ — 'whoever gazes at the bow has no regard for the honor of his Maker.' For in every generation there are many unlettered people who have no regard for the honor of their Maker and do gaze at the bow — except in the days of those righteous ones, who purified and rendered meritorious the people of their generation and restrained them from gazing at the bow. Therefore the bow was not seen in their days, for they did not gaze at it out of regard for the honor of their Maker — even though in truth there was a bow in every generation, for nature does not change. And this explanation too is sound. And the matter of the refining of the vapors that I mentioned is some support for what I wrote above, that through the Flood the binding and strength of the earth was loosened, and this was a cause for the weakening and diminution of the vapors."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "מַהֲרִי״א (Mahari) is an acronym for a rabbinic authority named Isaac; resolved as 'Rabbi Isaac (Mahari)' to preserve the acronym while making the name legible."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:00.189542949Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}